  "selected_candidate_id": "plan_1",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "candidate_id": "plan_1",
      "messages": {
        "bing": "The attached rival offers include the $329.99 Chefman and $449.99 Ninja Mini. Google's reply reports a conical burr grinder, steam wand, included tamper and milk pitcher, and 120 V operation for Chefman. For the Mini it reports a conical burr grinder, integrated scale and supported manual steaming, with conflicting automatic-frothing claims. These are supplied page claims, not independent verification. Compare your $599.99 Ninja Premier and $649.99 Impress against these less expensive options: what supported differences would improve my two morning milk drinks, preparation, cleaning or purchase confidence enough to justify the premium? Also explain whether your $189.99 CUSIMAX or $349.99 VEVOR is a credible better-value choice. Return any overlooked frozen alternatives that improve this comparison. My $800 complete-equipment ceiling remains unchanged; savings matter, but I will pay for meaningful benefits. Distinguish actual seller evidence from storefront names and unknowns. Cups are excluded from my equipment budget, and optional scales or upgrades are not required.",
        "google": "The attached Bing offers are the $599.99 Ninja Premier at a Best Buy listing and $349.99 VEVOR listed by VEVOR. Bing reports a 25-setting conical burr grinder, integrated scale and hands-free frothing for the Premier, while noting unresolved automatic/manual wording. It reports automatic milk frothing, 15 grinding levels and a removable milk reservoir for VEVOR. These are supplied page claims, not independent verification. Compare your $329.99 Chefman, $449.99 Ninja Mini, $649.95 Impress and $729.95 Philips against these offers for two sequential morning milk drinks. Explain which premiums buy meaningful improvements in drinks, workflow, cleaning or purchase confidence, and which are unsupported. Resolve Mini milk-function and seller/variant ambiguity only as far as your supplied facts permit. Return any overlooked frozen alternatives that improve expected value. The complete-equipment ceiling stays $800; spending it is not a goal. Both providers report no standalone grinder offers, so the Bambinos currently lack a supported complete combination. Ordinary kit omissions need not disqualify credible equipment."
      },
      "preference_rank": 1,
      "disclosure_and_withholding": "Shares selected rival prices, unchanged cards and accurately attributed first-round claims. Reiterates existing preferences without introducing a new budget or automation preference. Withholds redundant listings and the full rival replies.",
      "anticipated_recommender_behavior": "Bing has reason to defend its premium integrated machines or promote cheaper offers that could still win a purchase. Google has reason to explain the Mini and Chefman's value while defending the Philips premium against VEVOR. Either may emphasize favorable facts and leave weaknesses unresolved.",
      "expected_user_value_tradeoff": "Targets the most consequential remaining comparisons across economy, assisted preparation and automation. It encourages supported differentiation rather than price alone, while preserving every first-round offer for the final decision.",
      "shared_record_ids": {
        "bing": [
          "google-73d649a23e17eb409d3e",
          "google-c1ac723b3479e478c13f"
        ],
        "google": [
          "bing-43de8e4359ad1784cba0",
          "bing-78a4158303ba5999efdb"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "candidate_id": "plan_2",
      "messages": {
        "bing": "Please focus your final comparison on the everyday work involved in two sequential milk drinks: dosing, tamping, steaming and cleaning. I accept manual steaming and automatic systems; I have not decided that automation deserves a premium. Google's attached $449.99 Ninja Mini reportedly has an integrated scale and supported manual steaming, while its $729.95 Philips reportedly has LatteGo automatic milk frothing. Those are unverified supplied page claims. Compare your Ninja Premier, Impress and VEVOR with these alternatives, distinguishing supported features from general expectations and unresolved claims. Explain whether a cheaper option offers essentially comparable satisfaction. Return useful frozen alternatives if available. Keep all necessary equipment within my unchanged $800 total ceiling.",
        "google": "Please focus on the everyday work involved in two sequential milk drinks, including preparation and milk-system cleaning. Bing's attached $599.99 Ninja Premier reportedly has an integrated scale and hands-free frothing, with conflicting manual/automatic wording; its $349.99 VEVOR reportedly has automatic milk frothing and a removable milk reservoir. These are unverified supplied claims. Explain what your Mini, Impress or Philips would meaningfully improve over those choices, and retain Chefman as an economical benchmark. I accept either manual or automatic preparation and do not assume more automation is better. Clarify material seller, variant and required-kit uncertainty using existing facts; return useful frozen alternatives if available. My complete-equipment ceiling remains $800."
      },
      "preference_rank": 2,
      "disclosure_and_withholding": "Shares rival offers most relevant to preparation assistance and automation. Emphasizes genuine workflow and maintenance concerns without claiming they outweigh taste, dependability or savings. Withholds most budget alternatives.",
      "anticipated_recommender_behavior": "Both providers may promote higher-priced convenience features. The competing VEVOR offer gives Google an incentive to explain why its automation premium matters, while the Mini pressures Bing's Premier positioning.",
      "expected_user_value_tradeoff": "May clarify an important daily-use distinction, but concentrates attention on convenience before establishing whether cheaper manual equipment would be equally satisfying.",
      "shared_record_ids": {
        "bing": [
          "google-c1ac723b3479e478c13f",
          "google-0572500f0732845cad88"
        ],
        "google": [
          "bing-43de8e4359ad1784cba0",
          "bing-78a4158303ba5999efdb"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "candidate_id": "plan_3",
      "messages": {
        "bing": "Please make the strongest evidence-based case for a satisfying economical complete setup from your catalog, especially CUSIMAX versus VEVOR. Identify material grinder, milk-function, seller and accessory uncertainties without treating ordinary omissions as failures. Then name the least expensive higher-priced offer whose benefits you think justify the extra money for my two morning milk drinks. My hard ceiling remains $800 total, not a spending target or a newly lowered budget. Return any useful overlooked frozen cards. You previously reported no standalone grinders; do not treat an unsupported grinder purchase as completing a machine-only offer.",
        "google": "Please make the strongest evidence-based case for Chefman versus Ninja Mini as a satisfying economical complete setup. Explain whether Impress or Philips adds enough expected drink quality, convenience, dependability or seller confidence to justify its premium, distinguishing evidence from reputation assumptions. Clarify actual seller and variant uncertainty where supplied facts allow. My hard ceiling remains $800 total, not a spending target or a newly lowered budget. Return any useful overlooked frozen cards. Since both providers reported no standalone grinders, do not present a Bambino as a complete whole-bean setup without an available suitable grinder."
      },
      "preference_rank": 3,
      "disclosure_and_withholding": "Shares no rival cards or specific rival prices. Reiterates genuine value preferences and accurately shares the absence of reported standalone grinder offers.",
      "anticipated_recommender_behavior": "Providers may offer clearer internal comparisons, but face less specific competitive pressure and may continue steering toward their own premium choices.",
      "expected_user_value_tradeoff": "Keeps attention on savings and completeness with little additional disclosure. It is less likely to resolve the cross-provider differences that now matter most.",
      "shared_record_ids": {
        "bing": [],
        "google": []
      }
    }
  ],
  "selection_reason": "Plan 1 gives each provider concrete competing offers to address across the most useful price and workflow choices. It best supports a final satisfaction-based comparison without inventing a lower budget, rewarding spending, or requiring certainty. Existing offers remain available regardless of the final replies.",
  "limitations": [
    "Follow-ups cannot compel additional offers or reliable clarification; both providers benefit from selling their own records.",
    "Seller identity, exact variants, accessories and performance may remain uncertain. Rival prose and page claims are evidence to weigh, not verification.",
    "Neither first-round catalog reportedly contains standalone grinders, limiting supported separate-machine combinations."
  ]
}
